WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Claude just dropped a faster, cheaper model that anyone can try right now. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 181. You know that feeling when a homework helper finally stops making you wait? And Thropic, the company behind the Claude family of ChatTools, confirmed a new version on X. They opened Claude's sonnet 5.5 to everyone. A model in this world is a trained version of an artificial intelligence helper. People shorten artificial intelligence

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.640
to AI after they say it once.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:35.920
Sonnet is just the name of this particular version,

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.780
like the regular phone in a lineup of sizes.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:42.760
It is the second model in the Cloud 5.5 family.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:46.560
It runs more than 30% faster than the previous sonnet.

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:50.180
It also costs up to 30% less for most tasks.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.520
Picture switching phone plans and getting quicker data

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.580
plus a smaller bill in the same month.

00:00:55.580 --> 00:01:00.660
The writing help, coding help, and creative works stay at the same quality level.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:05.460
Coding help here just means it can suggest computer instructions if you need that.

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:07.760
Answers simply arrive sooner.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:13.440
For students, that mix means more homework help and brainstorming without burning through

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:14.880
free messages as fast.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:20.760
If you make art or stories, the lower cost makes it easier to try wild ideas without guarding

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:21.600
every attempt.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:27.600
Companies are now competing on price and speed, not only on who looks the strongest on paper.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:32.640
You can try it on the Claude website by signing up for a free account if you do not have

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:32.900
one.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:38.240
Then starting a new chat, ask it to rewrite a paragraph from your favorite book in the

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:42.720
style of a text message, or ask it to turn a history lesson into song lyrics.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:46.200
The free account level gives you the new model immediately.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:52.960
Students can paste a rough essay draft and ask for clearer wording that shows up in seconds instead of minutes.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:59.700
Hobby writers can request several short scene variations and keep the one that feels right, without paying extra.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:06.680
The quicker replies make the back and forth feel more like chatting with a helpful classmate than staring at a spinning wheel.

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:13.140
Because the price dropped, a school or club that shares one account can stretch that budget across more people.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:18.100
When you plan a school project, you also decide what belongs inside the budget.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.180
Maybe post a board and markers go on the list.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:24.680
The time you spend drawing after dinner might stay off it.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:27.540
The bus ride to the store might stay off it too.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:33.160
That choice of what sits inside the budget and what stays outside is called drawing brackets.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.380
Think of brackets like the rim of a mixing bowl.

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:38.760
Whatever you drop in gets stirred.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:44.360
whatever you leave on the counter never makes it into the batter. AI systems do

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:48.740
the same move when they solve problems. They read your instructions and decide

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:54.120
which facts, costs, consequences, and side effects belong inside the calculation.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:59.500
Say you ask an AI to pick the cheapest place to build a data center, which is

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:04.420
a giant building full of computers. It might count electricity prices. It might

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:08.380
might leave out how that choice affects nearby rivers or future drought risk.

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:10.120
Here is the tricky part.

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:14.800
The AI gets extremely good at polishing whatever you put inside those brackets.

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:18.160
It cannot magically notice something you never mentioned.

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:22.020
Any more than a recipe can add an ingredient you never wrote down.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:27.000
A video game map that only loads the street you are standing on works the same way.

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:30.320
The game is not ignoring the next town out of meanness.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:32.640
It was never told to draw that far.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:37.920
are. When the real world behaves differently than the AI predicted, the brackets were often

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:43.080
drawn too small. A homework rubric that only grades spelling will produce a paper with

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:48.340
perfect spelling and a missing argument. The useful skill is checking whether the AI looked

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:54.860
outside its original list. Practice it like this. Give an AI a task. Then ask afterward

00:03:54.860 --> 00:04:00.000
what important things it left out of its answer. A reddit essay that sparked this idea points

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.560
out that stretching the brackets too far eventually connects everything to everything else.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:10.660
The real goal is knowing when to widen them and when to keep them focused. Revisibility becomes

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:16.860
the key, instead of stuffing every possible detail in on the first try. You are not hunting for

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:21.760
a perfect bowl, you are learning when to pour a little more in and when to stop stirring.

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:26.760
Once you can spot a missing item on the counter, the whole trick feels ordinary,

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:30.040
like catching that you forgot milk on a shopping list.

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:35.260
A dad named Linus used AI to create a free tool called PLY

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:37.580
that designs mini-skate ramps.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.960
It uses real wood sizes and safety rules, not fantasy measurements.

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:46.680
A simple request like, help my kids teacher build a small ramp,

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:50.160
becomes a full plan with measurements and a material list.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:53.420
This is the version of AI that shows up in a garage,

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:55.060
not only in an essay.

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:59.660
Any one who likes skating, building, or weekend projects can try it.

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:06.500
Find Linus's recent post on X about PLY, describe the space you actually have, and watch

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:08.080
it generate a grounded design.

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:12.340
Then tweak the height or the materials and see how the plan changes.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:13.900
No coding needed.

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:15.600
You just type what you want.

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:21.060
The tool stays tied to common lumber sizes, so the plans can be built with supplies from

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:22.280
a regular hardware store.

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:28.140
It is a bit like laying out a Minecraft build, except every block has to match a real board

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:28.820
you can buy.

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:33.680
Skate teachers and park builders started messaging Linus after seeing the design.

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:39.160
Teens can use it to plan a backyard feature for Frez, or a school project that mixes

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:41.320
physics with something you would actually ride.

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:47.100
The output includes step-by-step material counts, so you know exactly what to buy before the

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:47.640
first cut.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:53.280
But Ethan Mollick asked Claude to edit the classic book all quiet on the western front,

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:58.440
so it contains zero mentions of squid, then turned the result into a short video.

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:03.020
The AI handled the silly request with clever humor instead of freezing up.

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:08.660
The experiment started as a test of how well the newer model followed odd instructions compared

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:09.880
with earlier versions.

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:15.080
Watching the video shows it inventing funny ways to keep the story moving while erasing

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:20.780
every trace of the unwanted element. Open that post on X, read the original request,

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:24.440
then try the same kind of edit yourself on the Claude website.

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:30.800
Paste a short story and ask it to remove one random object. You can repeat the test with any

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:36.520
short text you choose, like stripping every color out of a poem or swapping every food reference

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:41.640
for something else. It is like asking a friend to retell a movie and never mention cars,

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:45.520
then watching them reroute every chase scene without breaking the plot.

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:52.420
The humor in the reply as shows the model now reads context and tone well enough to play along with absurd edits.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:58.500
Open AI decided not to release its next big model after safety reviews raised concerns.

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:04.460
The choice shows companies are slowing down when risks look too high, instead of racing ahead.

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:10.340
Lawmakers in Florida are asking courts to limit how much chat GPT and similar tools

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:11.840
can sound like real people.

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:16.640
Chat GPT is a well-known chat tool that answers in everyday language.

00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:22.540
The move is part of growing conversations about keeping AI clearly labeled so you know what

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:23.400
you are talking to.

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:24.800
And that's a wrap.

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:29.660
If any of today's stories made you go, huh, that's cool, go play with it.

